{"name":"Sweet Blue","hex":"#aebed2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/1280px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boys Pilfering Molasses – On The Quays, New Orleans, 1853 painting by George Henry Hall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sweetness is perceived by the taste buds.","link":"https: